WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:12.369
The breaking of encryption was essential to the war effort.

00:00:12.369 --> 00:00:17.363
England would probably have fallen if they'd not been able to read the Enigma messages,

00:00:17.363 --> 00:00:23.609
because every time the Germans sent bombers, the RAF knew where they were sending them,

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:26.220
and could mask the planes there to defend.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:32.390
If any significant fraction of those attacks had gotten through, England would have been much more helpless,

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:36.710
may well have fallen and the course of World War II may have changed.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:44.390
And that is the origin of the regulations that said, "This is ammunition, this is an item of war."

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.957
And the problem was, they didn't really take freedom of speech,

00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:54.469
freedom of inquiry, academic freedom, into account in that.

00:00:54.469 --> 00:01:08.587
But on the matter of encryption, terrorists
are going to have greater access.

00:01:08.587 --> 00:01:14.416
Coming up: the story of the U.S. Government's long battle to keep strong encryption out of the hands of its citizens,

00:01:14.416 --> 00:01:17.295
and how First Amendment activists
fought back.

00:01:17.295 --> 00:01:21.961
Here's part three in a four-part series about how the cypherpunk movement of the 1990s

00:01:21.961 --> 00:01:27.136
 anticipated the promise and the peril that lay ahead, when the internet upended the world.

00:01:34.245 --> 00:01:41.060
In 1977, researchers at MIT made an astonishing discovery, a mathematical system for encrypting

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:47.639
secret messages so powerful that it had the potential to make government spying effectively impossible.

00:01:47.639 --> 00:01:50.757
But before these mathematicians could publish their research,

00:01:50.757 --> 00:01:55.379
the NSA made it clear that doing so could land them in Federal prison.

00:01:55.379 --> 00:02:00.410
The U.S. Government considered these mathematical systems modern weapons technologies

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:05.530
and distributing them was a crime under the International Traffic in Arms Regulations.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.681
MIT halted plans to distribute the paper.

00:02:08.681 --> 00:02:15.374
I decided quite literally, they are going
to classify this over my dead body.

00:02:15.374 --> 00:02:20.930
Mark Miller was a 20 year old student at Yale, who had read about this discovery in Scientific American.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:26.298
So I traveled to MIT, managed to get my hand
on a paper copy of this thing.

00:02:26.298 --> 00:02:32.074
I went to a variety of different copy shops so I wasn't making lots of copies in any one place.

00:02:32.074 --> 00:02:38.064
I sent them to home and hobbyist computer
organizations and magazines all across the country.

00:02:38.064 --> 00:02:43.361
And I gave copies of the paper to some select
friends of mine.

00:02:43.361 --> 00:02:51.532
And I told them if I disappear, make sure
this gets out.

00:02:51.532 --> 00:02:58.190
Early in 1978, the U.S. federal government backed off and decided to allow the publication.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:03.275
And the world has been on a different course
ever since it got published.

00:03:03.275 --> 00:03:05.530
But the Crypto Wars were just getting started.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.760
In the late 70s, the National Security Agency
declared the unrestrained public discussion

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.345
of cryptographic research threatened our national
security.

00:03:14.345 --> 00:03:21.250
The private cryptography community and the government have been sparring over the issue ever since.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.491
Marshall McLuhan said that one day we would
all live in a global village.

00:03:25.491 --> 00:03:30.510
Well, the global village has arrived and it's
main street is called the internet.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:35.955
The internet also created an electronic surveillance
apparatus that was like nothing the world had ever seen,

00:03:35.955 --> 00:03:41.242
which meant that the cryptography breakthroughs of the 1970s were no longer a rarified topic.

00:03:41.242 --> 00:03:43.421
They were privacy salvation.

00:03:43.421 --> 00:03:46.924
Internet users could shield their online activities from spying,

00:03:46.924 --> 00:03:53.230
and that sent the intelligence community once again scrambling to stop the dissemination of this powerful tool.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:56.604
In 1991, a software developer named Phil Zimmermann

00:03:56.604 --> 00:04:04.150
released the first relatively easy to use secret messaging system built with strong encryption, which was called PGP.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:09.190
Somehow it got overseas. Now that it's over there, people are using it and I'm delighted to hear good stories

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:13.827
about how it's used in places where there
are oppressive governments.

00:04:13.827 --> 00:04:18.250
So the U.S. justice department launched a
three year criminal investigation of Zimmermann

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.870
on the grounds that by making his software
accessible outside the country, 

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:26.300
he could be guilty of exporting weapons under the Arms Control Act.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.870
Meanwhile, the National Security Agency argued that Zimmermann software

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.514
would be used by child molesters and criminals. 

00:04:32.514 --> 00:04:38.119
"PGP, they say is out there to protect freedom fighters in Latvia" said the NSA's general counsel. 

00:04:38.119 --> 00:04:42.167
But the fact is the only use that has come
to the attention of law enforcement agencies

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:47.501
is a guy who was using PGP so the police could
not tell what little boys he had seduced over the net. 

00:04:47.501 --> 00:04:51.210
Child pornographers, terrorists, money launderers, take your pick.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:55.144
These are the people who will be invoked as
the bringers of death and destruction.

00:04:55.144 --> 00:04:58.666
Well, it's true. It's true.

00:04:58.666 --> 00:04:59.884
It's true.

00:04:59.884 --> 00:05:02.961
So they're right? Yes.

00:05:02.961 --> 00:05:08.637
But all technologies have had bad effects, the telephone, extortion, death threats, bomb threats,

00:05:08.637 --> 00:05:14.630
kidnapping cases, uncontrolled publishing
of books could allow satanic books to appear.

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:19.675
The cypherpunks argued that PGP was like any other published material because under the hood,

00:05:19.675 --> 00:05:23.330
it was just a series of instructions
to be carried out by a machine.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:27.886
"Restraint on freedom of expression of software writers is anathema in a free society, 

00:05:27.886 --> 00:05:32.266
and a violation of the First Amendment" wrote the economist and entrepreneur Phil Salin

00:05:32.266 --> 00:05:36.419
 in 1991 essay titled Freedom of Speech in Software.

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:42.150
Encryption can't be controlled whether or
not it's powerful or has impacts on the government,

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.449
because it's free speech.

00:05:44.449 --> 00:05:49.830
In the early 90s, John Gilmore, who was a co-founder of the cypherpunk movement risked going to jail

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:55.050
in his campaign to force the government to acknowledge that regulating encryption violated the First Amendment.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:06:01.013
We basically had a community of a thousand people scattered around who were all trying different ideas

00:06:01.013 --> 00:06:06.350
on how to get around the government to get encryption to the masses.

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:11.525
What we're afraid of several years from now is the terrorists both hit their encryption button on the telephone.

00:06:11.525 --> 00:06:16.380
And even though we have access, we can't understand what they're saying on a real-time basis.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:22.279
The Clinton administration told Congress that Americans have no constitutional right to choose their own method of encryption 

00:06:22.279 --> 00:06:28.660
and push for legislation that would require companies to build in a mechanism for law enforcement agencies to break in.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:31.770
We're in favor of strong encryption, robust
encryption.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:36.800
We just want to make sure we have a trap door
and a key under some judge's authority 

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.270
where we can get there if somebody is planning a crime.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:45.000
The cypherpunks looked for ways to undercut the government's case by pointing out the similarities

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.786
between encryption software and other forms of protected speech.

00:06:48.786 --> 00:06:55.000
With Phil Zimmermann under federal investigation for making his software available for download outside the U.S.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.040
To prove a point he convinced MIT press to mirror his action in the analog world,

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:05.460
by printing out the PGP source code, adding a binding, and shipping it to European bookstores.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:10.000
The government knew if they went to court to suppress the publication of a book

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.900
 from a university, that they would lose and they would lose in a hurry.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:20.502
There were people who actually got encryption code tattooed on their bodies and then started asking,

00:07:20.502 --> 00:07:23.130
"Can I go to a foreign country?"

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:30.790
We printed up T-shirts that had encryption code on them and submitted them to the government Office of Munitions Control.

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:33.780
Can we publish this T-shirt?

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:39.020
Ultimately, they never answered that query because they realized to say no would be

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:43.790
to invite a lawsuit they would lose and so the best answer was no answer at all.

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:46.288
Tell us a little bit more about encryption
technology?

00:07:46.288 --> 00:07:50.220
No, no.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:51.389
Thank you.

00:07:51.389 --> 00:07:56.647
I hesitated even to make, use the word, but
I'm not using it anymore, right?

00:07:56.647 --> 00:08:02.329
In 1996, the justice department announced that it wouldn't pursue criminal charges against Phil Zimmermann,

00:08:02.329 --> 00:08:08.180
and major court victories came when two federal judges found that encryption is protected by the First Amendment.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:10.539
The Crypto Wars are still ongoing.

00:08:10.539 --> 00:08:17.080
What we won in the first rounds was the right to publish it and the right to put it in mass market software

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:21.710
but what we didn't actually do is deploy it in mass market software.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:27.809
Now there are major companies building serious
encryption into their products,

00:08:27.809 --> 00:08:30.871
and we're getting a lot of pushback from the government about this.

00:08:30.871 --> 00:08:36.139
We're about the rule of law, but we don't want to create spaces that are beyond the breach of the law in the United States, right?

00:08:36.139 --> 00:08:40.687
It's not just the reprehensible behavior of
sexual predation on children, 

00:08:40.687 --> 00:08:48.110
but myriad additional forms of serious crime enabled by end to end encryption.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:52.490
I'm not optimistic that privacy will prevail in the Crypto Wars.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:55.339
It's an ongoing struggle.

00:08:55.339 --> 00:09:01.360
In the early 90s, at the same time that Gilmore was fighting his legal battle for freedom of speech in software,

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:06.984
the cypherpunks were exploring cryptography's potential in the context of collapsing political borders

00:09:06.984 --> 00:09:09.069
and the rise of liberal democracy.

00:09:09.069 --> 00:09:14.522
Part four in this series, we'll look at how those dreams turn to disillusionment, the death of the cypherpunk movement,

00:09:14.522 --> 00:09:17.630
and its rebirth following the invention of Bitcoin.

